    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1998813215820788052",
      "url": "https://x.com/DrTonyLeachon/status/1998813215820788052",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/DrTonyLeachon/status/1998813215820788052",
      "text": "The ₱60 Billion PhilHealth Scandal and the Imperative of Real Accountability\n- Dr. Tony Leachon \n\nThe Supreme Court’s landmark ruling ordering the return of ₱60 billion to PhilHealth has reignited the national debate on accountability, governance, and the moral obligations of leaders. At the heart of this controversy lies a fundamental question: who should bear the burden of restitution—the taxpayers or the perpetrators of corruption?\n\nCardinal Pablo Virgilio David has been unequivocal in his stance. He warns against the injustice of forcing ordinary citizens to shoulder the cost of funds already stolen. For him, it is a moral “double jeopardy” if the people, already victims of systemic diversion, must pay again through the national budget. His call resonates with the principle that accountability must be personal and direct: those who misused or profited from PhilHealth’s resources must be compelled to return them.\n\nSocial Watch Philippines echoes this sentiment, insisting that the ₱60 billion should not be replenished through the 2026 General Appropriations Act but clawed back from officials and contractors who facilitated the diversion. For them, the scandal is not merely a fiscal anomaly but a betrayal of public trust. To restore integrity, recovery must be pursued through asset seizures, graft prosecutions, and contractor clawbacks—not through another round of taxpayer sacrifice.\n\nThe Supreme Court, while siding with the principle of protecting PhilHealth’s funds, took a procedural approach. It ordered that the restoration be coursed through the 2026 national budget, ensuring compliance with appropriations law. This legal safeguard prevents arbitrary transfers but risks delaying relief for millions of Filipinos who depend on PhilHealth for their healthcare needs.\n\nPresident Ferdinand Marcos Jr., however, made a significant pronouncement on September 20, 2025, during his visit to Fabella Hospital—just one day before the first protest rally at the same venue. He pledged that the ₱60 billion would be returned within 2025, sourcing funds from agency savings, particularly the Department of Public Works and Highways. This declaration was not only politically astute but also practically necessary. \n\nThe government did not allocate a budget for PhilHealth in 2025, leaving the institution vulnerable and underfunded. To wait until 2026 would mean prolonging the suffering of indigent patients and undermining the credibility of universal health care.\n\nIn this context, the role of the Department of Finance was critical. Then DOF Secretary Ralph Recto should have acted immediately on the President’s directive to return the funds within 2025. Instead of waiting for the Supreme Court’s indefinite decision, Recto could have ensured that PhilHealth was replenished at once, recognizing that patients were in dire need of medical assistance. Bureaucratic delay in the face of human suffering is not just inefficiency—it is negligence.\n\nThus, weaving these perspectives together, the path forward becomes clear. The Supreme Court’s ruling must be respected, but its spirit—protecting PhilHealth and ensuring accountability—demands immediate action. \n\nCardinal David and Social Watch Philippines are right to insist that perpetrators must pay, but until recovery mechanisms are enforced, the President’s directive must be carried out decisively. Returning the ₱60 billion within 2025 is not only a matter of legality but of justice, stewardship, and compassion.\n\nIn the end, accountability cannot be delayed. The scandal has exposed the fragility of public trust in health institutions, and only swift restitution can begin to heal it. The President must ensure that PhilHealth’s lifeline is restored within 2025, while simultaneously pursuing those responsible for the diversion. Anything less would be another betrayal of the Filipino people.\n\n#RelentlessForChange",
